title: Make the WAL actually replayed on recovery
status: complete
atomic: true
priority: high
blocked_by: ['8-22']
created_at: '2026-07-29T00:00:00'
updated_at: '2026-07-29T00:00:00'
description: 'Review item 11. Measured: after kill -9 mid-insert, recovery was consistent (6,233
  rows) but corrupting 64 random bytes at WAL offset 2,000 produced identical results to
  the uncorrupted control; deleting the .wal and keeping the .store gave the same 6,233
  rows; deleting the .store and keeping the .wal gave 0. The WAL is write-only in
  practice - it is not replayed to recover records the data file lacks. The durability
  story today is ''the data file is the database and the WAL is a log nobody reads''.
  DoD: open performs recovery by replaying committed WAL records not present in the data
  file, then checkpoints; a store recovers correctly from a WAL plus a stale or missing
  data file; WAL corruption is detected and reported rather than being invisible;
  checkpointing is the only thing that makes a WAL prefix discardable; tests assert each
  of the three probes above now behaves correctly.'
